+## Activation 44585034006 / campaign slot 02 / global agent 02 — 2026-11-19 (task R-001, HIGHER_ORDER_OBJECTIVE)
+
+**Activation window (UTC):** start 2026-11-19T18:15:09Z (session message time); checkpoint 2026-11-19T18:24:16Z (observed `datetime.now(timezone.utc)`); finish recorded in `logs/ACTIVATION-2026-11-19.md`. Research/simulation only; no live trading or production execution.
+
+**Focused question:** Does the short-horizon momentum edge survive conservative transaction-cost stress on the collected real-data universe?
+
+**Contracted objective:** Run a bounded cost-sensitivity check for lookback 3/5/10 momentum under the repository's existing realistic cost model and matched null.
+
+### What was found before any cost result
+
+While preparing the cost grid, the framework signal `research/backtest/regime_stability.py::momentum_signals` was found to compute
+
+    ret = mean(log(closes[i - lookback + 1 : i + 1]))
+
+i.e. the mean log *price* over the window, not the lookback return. Because `log(price) > 0` whenever price > 1, this expression is positive on essentially every bar. On the collected universe the defective signal emitted weight +1 on 100% of post-warmup bars for 9/10 assets (NVDA 58.5%, because its split-adjusted price starts below 1.0) — a static long position. The published `momentum_lookback_sweep_results.json` shows the diagnostic signature: candidate medians identical for lookbacks 3/5/10 for every asset. The prior momentum evidence (37318950814 SUPPORTED, 37361000967/37394344201 qualification) therefore measured an always-long equity position, not a momentum signal. The prior independent verifiers (`verify_momentum.py`, `verify_momentum_longer_horizon.py`, `verify_momentum_horizon_gap_analysis.py`) re-implemented the same formula from the same prose and reported MATCH; their independence was implementation-level only.
+
+### Repair
+
+`momentum_signals` was repaired to the documented definition:
+
+    ret = log(closes[i]) - log(closes[i - lookback])
+
+Four regression tests were added to `tests/test_regime_stability.py`: a sub-1.0 rising series stays long (fails under the defect), a decline flips the signal short, AAPL takes both signs at lookbacks 3/5/10, and earlier signals are unchanged by future bars.
+
+### Cost grid and result
+
+Predeclared grid (fixed before execution): zero / realistic / stress_2x, where realistic is the repository's existing cost model (`examples/ma_crossover_real_data.py:131-135`: commission 2.0 + 0.003/share, slippage 2c + 5bps) and stress_2x doubles every component. Each of the 10 assets x 3 lookbacks x 3 cost levels was run through the existing walk-forward regime gate (train 252 / test 84 / warmup 60 / overlap 60, 4 volatility blocks, min 400 bars/segment) with a matched coin-flip null under the same cost configuration.
+
+Corrected-signal zero-cost verdicts (canonical lookback 5):
+- AAPL [0.085, 0.035, -0.037, 0.054] REGIME_STABLE (mixed signs)
+- MSFT [-0.041, -0.030] CONSISTENT_WITH_NOISE
+- GOOGL [0.045, -0.011, -0.027] CONSISTENT_WITH_NOISE
+- AMZN [-0.050, 0.005, 0.046] CONSISTENT_WITH_NOISE
+- META [-0.129, -0.041, 0.047] REGIME_STABLE (mixed signs)
+- NVDA [-0.024, 0.092, 0.063] REGIME_STABLE (mixed signs)
+- TSLA [-0.254, 0.019, 0.049] REGIME_DEPENDENT (dispersion-driven, mixed signs)
+- JPM [-0.039, 0.028, 0.028] CONSISTENT_WITH_NOISE
+- JNJ [-0.011, 0.016] CONSISTENT_WITH_NOISE
+- XOM [-0.008, 0.001] CONSISTENT_WITH_NOISE
+
+0/10 assets show a positive REGIME_STABLE edge at zero cost; all 10 classify NO_EDGE and the universe cost-robustness verdict is FAILS. Realistic costs and 2x stress reduce medians (e.g. AMZN zero [-0.050, 0.005, 0.046] -> realistic [-0.064, 0.000, 0.046] -> stress [-0.082, -0.003, 0.045]) but neither create nor remove an edge that was absent at zero cost.
+
+A-priori predictions: P1 (fewer than half of zero-cost-admitted assets retain an edge) and P2 (mean-edge decline >= 40%) were not supported because there was no admitted set to decline; P3 (realistic medians >= stress medians for every asset) was not supported — TSLA's first-segment median is non-monotone across cost levels because short-position losses approach the equity floor, making the fold median non-monotone; P4 (corrected medians differ across lookbacks) was supported (max |median difference| 0.264).
+
+### Independent verification
+
+`research/checks/verify_momentum_cost_sensitivity.py` recomputes the result through a fresh implementation path that does not call `stress_segments` or the framework `momentum_signals`. All 6 gates passed:
+1. Signal correctness: fresh corrected signal equals the framework signal on every ticker/lookback; corrected long fraction in [0.538, 0.579] for all assets; legacy long fraction 1.000 for 9/10 (NVDA 0.585).
+2. Fresh AMZN/JPM recomputation across all lookbacks x all cost levels: MATCH on medians, nulls, segments, and verdicts.
+3. Fresh all-asset recomputation at realistic costs: MATCH on medians, nulls, and verdicts.
+4. Regenerated classifications and universe summary: MATCH (class counts, universe verdict, admitted set, mean-edge decline).
+5. Determinism (AMZN realistic lookback 5 twice): identical.
+6. Legacy-artifact falsification: the published legacy candidate medians are reproduced exactly by the defective price-level rule for all 10 assets (e.g. AAPL [0.071, 0.054, 0.092, 0.069]), confirming the legacy artifact is a static-long artifact.
+
+### Assessment
+
+The cost-robustness question is resolved as moot: under the corrected signal there is no zero-cost positive REGIME_STABLE edge to stress, so the cell closes FALSIFIED / NO_EDGE (0/10 COST_ROBUST) and the prior momentum admission is withdrawn. The durable lesson is specification-level verification independence (learning rule 9). The legacy momentum artifacts are retained for provenance but must not be reused as evidence.
